WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.070
I'm not allowed to climb this tower.

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:03.930
I did ask, but not only does climbing this

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:06.150
need proper training and safety equipment,

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:08.130
it's also a protected historic site.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:11.790
The folks in charge are
understandably a bit cautious.

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:13.628
But while I can't go up there...

00:00:13.628 --> 00:00:15.972
the camera and you can.

00:00:15.972 --> 00:00:18.789
This is a moonlight tower
here in Austin, Texas,

00:00:18.789 --> 00:00:22.080
and it's one of the last surviving
municipal lighting towers anywhere,

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.840
because if you're lighting up a city
to keep people safe at night,

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.900
why have thousands of small lamps
down every street

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:31.980
when you could have just a few,
very bright and very high?

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:37.666
- Each tower was intended to provide light
equal to that of natural moonlight

00:00:37.666 --> 00:00:41.640
in an area 1500 feet
from the base of the tower,

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.690
and that is light by which somebody could
read the face of their watch.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:49.020
These lighting systems were
all throughout the Midwest

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:50.820
and all throughout the west.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:53.190
- When these towers
were installed in 1894,

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:57.030
they used carbon arc lamps,
the first practical electric lights.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:00.150
Those lamps were literally
controlled electrical arcing,

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:04.200
a constant spark shooting through the air
between two carbon electrodes.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.510
These days, if you're seeing that kind of
harsh white light at this scale, 

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:10.830
it's usually because there's a fault
with something big and electrical

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:12.540
and there's a short in the system.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:14.880
This was the same effect, just deliberate.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.340
The catch is that the
electrodes on carbon arc lamps

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:20.850
are steadily vaporized, wearing down
as they're worn away by the spark,

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:24.120
so they burn out quickly.
And the city had to pay someone

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:27.510
to climb every tower every day
to replace the lamps.

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:30.990
- The City of Austin Electric Utility,
and as we know it today, Austin Energy,

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:33.090
started with the moonlight towers.

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:36.540
Every night, a utility employee
would go up

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:40.380
through this elevator pulley system
through the middle of the tower

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:42.660
and go light these carbon arc lamps.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.000
This pulley system,
there's this little thing

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.040
for the employee to stand in,

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.030
and then you have workers on the ground
who are hoisting him up.

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:54.360
You're going up potentially
165 feet in the air.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:57.330
I don't know when hard hats
came into play,

00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:59.070
but when you look at
older pictures,

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:02.160
our safety equipment
is not as it is today.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:05.160
So then in the 1920s, they
switched to incandescent bulbs,

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:08.040
and in 1936 it was the
mercury vapor lamps,

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:09.690
and today we're using LED bulbs,

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:12.300
and it's a little bit
easier to get up there

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:13.680
and exchange the parts.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.570
So we're using cranes and bucket trucks

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:18.990
to get up to make those changes.

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:20.220
- If ever there was a subject

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.560
designed for vertical
video, it's this one.

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:24.450
Most cities took their towers down

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:26.940
as electric street lighting
became possible.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:30.660
For some unknown reason,
Austin never got round to it,

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:32.310
but they've not all been preserved.

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:34.530
There's only about a dozen
towers still standing.

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:37.140
Not all of those are in the
same locations they used to be.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:40.140
And in 1993, the city took them all apart,

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.480
refurbished them, and
put them back up again.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:44.730
As for what they look like
when they're switched on,

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.490
well, for that, we'll need
to wait until after sunset.

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:49.890
- Austin is the only city
in the United States

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:51.660
that still has functioning moon towers.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.360
Before the moonlight towers
were put up around town,

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:56.370
it was dark!
The moon could only do so much.

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:57.870
And so it really changed people's lives.

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:00.900
I think it allowed them to keep
businesses open after dark,

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:02.340
which would increase commerce.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:04.980
People could enjoy themselves
with friends and family,

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:06.510
and also get home safely.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:08.220
Moonlight towers were
the only game in town

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:10.475
for light after dark, you know,

00:03:10.475 --> 00:03:13.260
in parallel with the
moon itself, of course.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:14.580
- And these towers clearly work

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:16.980
because, well, look at
me, I've got a shadow,

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:19.050
although admittedly I am
standing right next to it.

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:22.020
The name moonlight towers
isn't just poetic.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:23.730
31 of these towers were spread around

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:26.490
what was then a much
smaller and lower city

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:28.200
before skyscrapers came along.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:30.960
That meant that except
for some dark corners,

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.000
most of the town would be lit up

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.340
as if there was a full
or maybe a half moon.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:36.780
Not that bright,

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:38.970
but enough that you could
see your way around.

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:42.060
Hence moonlight tower.
It's artificial moonlight.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:43.800
Although, turns out
there's not much evidence

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:46.290
for that name being used
until the mid-20th century

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:48.600
when historical preservation
became fashionable

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.960
and these towers became an icon of Austin.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.840
- They were not called moonlight
towers from the beginning.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:58.500
The Austin newspapers just
called these "the towers".

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:00.210
There were no other towers.

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:03.000
First reference that
I found in a newspaper

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:04.697
to these being called moonlight towers,

00:04:04.697 --> 00:04:06.840
it was 1938.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:08.370
In the early 1960s,

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:10.650
the masthead of the Austin newspaper

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:13.590
had the phrase, "As Austin As".

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:15.930
The phrase changed each day,

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:17.970
where the newspaper was comparing itself

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:20.460
to other icons in the city of Austin.

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:24.337
And in July, 1962, the masthead said,

00:04:24.337 --> 00:04:27.090
"As Austin as Moonlight Towers".

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:29.640
They have become a symbol of Austin.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:31.050
- And every holiday season,

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:33.240
one of those moonlight
towers in Zilker Park

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:36.750
gets used to put up hundreds
and hundreds of bulbs of lights

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:39.120
that look like a holiday tree.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:41.460
And you go and you spin
around and you look up

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:43.650
and you get dizzy, and
you feel like Austin

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:45.273
is just the best place to be.

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:48.480
- There are modern
versions of these towers

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:50.430
for places like giant parking lots.

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:54.450
Sometimes it is just better to
stick a big light on a pole.

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:58.179
In most cases, though, modern lighting
is a much better choice,

00:04:58.179 --> 00:05:01.184
because every street got
wired up to the grid anyway.

00:05:01.184 --> 00:05:03.485
Although that does mean
in the 21st century,

00:05:03.485 --> 00:05:07.582
it's difficult to imagine what a city lit just by
moonlight towers would feel like.

00:05:07.582 --> 00:05:09.677
These days there's too
much other light around

00:05:09.677 --> 00:05:11.243
from every house, every building.

00:05:11.243 --> 00:05:12.339
On a night with low cloud,

00:05:12.339 --> 00:05:14.162
the skyglow from light pollution

00:05:14.162 --> 00:05:15.771
can be brighter than
the old towers would be.

00:05:15.771 --> 00:05:17.551
At least, brighter than they
would be at a distance.

00:05:17.565 --> 00:05:19.274
These aren't street lighting anymore.

00:05:19.274 --> 00:05:23.307
They're historic artifacts,
kept running because, well,

00:05:23.307 --> 00:05:25.680
because the people of Austin like them.

